WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.171
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.171 --> 00:00:04.662
<v Examiner>Describe the primary survey, A to E,

3
00:00:04.662 --> 00:00:09.090
<v Examiner>and give the normal adult range for each vital sign it includes.

4
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.227
The primary survey gives the most important information first,

5
00:00:13.227 --> 00:00:15.129
in order of clinical importance.

6
00:00:15.579 --> 00:00:15.993
Airway.

7
00:00:15.993 --> 00:00:17.766
Can the patient talk normally?

8
00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:18.652
Can they cough?

9
00:00:18.652 --> 00:00:20.603
Can you see anything blocking it?

10
00:00:20.603 --> 00:00:22.376
Are they anxious, or drooling?

11
00:00:22.376 --> 00:00:25.094
An airway obstruction can kill within minutes.

12
00:00:25.544 --> 00:00:26.175
Breathing.

13
00:00:26.175 --> 00:00:29.900
Respiratory rate, normal twelve to twenty breaths a minute.

14
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.677
Count it while you appear to take the pulse,

15
00:00:32.677 --> 00:00:37.223
so the patient doesn't change their breathing: thirty seconds times two,

16
00:00:37.223 --> 00:00:39.432
or a full minute if it's irregular.

17
00:00:39.432 --> 00:00:42.841
Under twelve is bradypnoea, over twenty is tachypnoea.

18
00:00:42.841 --> 00:00:48.207
Check depth, sounds like stridor or wheeze, and the work of breathing: nasal flaring,

19
00:00:48.207 --> 00:00:50.353
indrawing, tracheal tug, cyanosis.

20
00:00:50.353 --> 00:00:54.267
Oxygen saturation, normal ninety-five to one hundred per cent.

21
00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:55.537
Circulation.

22
00:00:55.537 --> 00:01:00.525
Blood pressure: systolic one-ten to one-forty, diastolic sixty to ninety.

23
00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:03.394
The first Korotkoff sound is the systolic.

24
00:01:03.394 --> 00:01:09.544
Pain, anxiety, exertion, white coat syndrome, age, medicines and blood loss all change it.

25
00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:15.488
Pulse: sixty to a hundred beats a minute, checking rate, rhythm, strength and equality.

26
00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:18.152
If it's irregular, count a full minute.

27
00:01:18.152 --> 00:01:20.475
Never check both carotids at once.

28
00:01:20.925 --> 00:01:23.465
Disability: level of consciousness.

29
00:01:23.465 --> 00:01:26.440
AVPU is Alert, Voice, Pain, Unresponsive.

30
00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:31.446
The Glasgow Coma Scale scores eye opening, verbal and motor response,

31
00:01:31.446 --> 00:01:33.043
from three to fifteen.

32
00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:39.103
Exposure: temperature, normal thirty-six point five to thirty-seven point five degrees.

33
00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:42.399
Two more number sets the themes list names.

34
00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:46.900
Blood glucose: normal four to seven point five millimoles per litre,

35
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:48.554
and under four is a hypo.

36
00:01:48.554 --> 00:01:53.319
Injections: intramuscular at ninety degrees, subcutaneous at forty-five,

37
00:01:53.319 --> 00:01:58.746
or ninety if you can grasp five centimetres of tissue, and intradermal at fifteen.

38
00:01:58.746 --> 00:02:03.048
The ventrogluteal site is safe for all patients, up to four mils.

39
00:02:03.048 --> 00:02:05.364
The deltoid takes two mils or less.

40
00:02:05.364 --> 00:02:08.938
The abdomen is the usual site for insulin and heparin.

41
00:02:09.388 --> 00:02:13.181
Primary survey: airway; breathing, twelve to twenty,

42
00:02:13.181 --> 00:02:16.973
saturations ninety-five to one hundred; circulation,

43
00:02:16.973 --> 00:02:23.100
B P one-ten to one-forty over sixty to ninety, pulse sixty to a hundred; disability,

44
00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.163
AVPU and G C S three to fifteen; exposure,

45
00:02:26.163 --> 00:02:29.737
thirty-six point five to thirty-seven point five.

46
00:02:29.737 --> 00:02:32.144
Glucose four to seven point five.

47
00:02:32.144 --> 00:02:37.322
Injections: I M ninety degrees, subcut forty-five, intradermal fifteen.
